CAT = "s65StateLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
('A client pledges stock as collateral for a bona fide bank loan. Under the Uniform Securities Act, the pledge is:', 'Not a sale', ['A sale by the client', 'An offer to the bank', 'An exempt sale'], 'The Act says a bona fide pledge or loan is not a sale or offer, so no registration questions arise. Selling the stock to repay the loan would be a sale.'),
('A company gives shareholders a stock dividend for which they pay nothing. Under the Uniform Securities Act, this is:', 'Not a sale', ['A sale of new stock', 'An exempt offer', 'A taxable offer'], 'A stock dividend with nothing of value given by the shareholder is not a sale or offer. A dividend that required shareholders to pay something would be.'),
('A shareholder gives away assessable stock, on which the company may levy more payments. Under the Act, the gift is:', 'Treated as a sale', ['Never a securities matter', 'An exempt transaction', 'A pledge'], 'The Uniform Securities Act treats a gift of assessable stock as a sale and offer, because the recipient takes on a duty to pay future assessments.'),
('A company sells shares to residents of a state under Rule 506 of Regulation D. A state that requires a Form D notice filing generally wants it:', 'Within 15 days of the first sale there', ['Before any offer in the state', 'Within 90 days of the first sale', 'Only after the offering closes'], 'Rule 506 securities are federal covered, so states cannot require registration, only a notice filing. NASAA states generally ask for the Form D, a fee and consent to service of process no later than 15 days after the first sale in the state.'),
('An investor sells older shares of a company to another investor through a broker. This is:', 'A nonissuer transaction', ['An issuer transaction', 'A primary offering', 'A private placement'], 'In a nonissuer transaction the proceeds do not go to the issuer, as in ordinary secondary trading. An issuer transaction raises money for the company.'),
("Many states exempt nonissuer trades in a security whose issuer's information appears in a recognized securities manual. This is called the:", 'Manual exemption', ['Isolated trade exemption', 'Coordination exemption', 'Federal covered exemption'], 'The manual exemption covers secondary trades in securities of issuers whose basic financial information is published in a recognized securities manual.'),
('A company offers new shares only to its existing shareholders, and no commission is paid for soliciting them. Under the Act, this is generally:', 'An exempt transaction', ['A nonissuer sale', 'An exempt security', 'A sale needing qualification'], 'Offers to existing security holders, including holders of convertible securities, are an exempt transaction when no commission is paid for the solicitation.'),
('Under the Uniform Securities Act, which of these is NOT a security?', 'A fixed annuity contract', ['A variable annuity contract', 'A collateral trust certificate', 'A profit-sharing agreement interest'], "The Act's definition excludes insurance, endowment and annuity contracts under which an insurer promises a fixed sum. Variable annuities, collateral trust certificates and profit-sharing interests are all securities."),
('For a state-registered adviser with custody of client funds or discretion, the Administrator may require:', 'A minimum net worth or bond', ['A guaranteed client return', 'SEC registration instead', 'A FINRA membership'], 'The Act lets the Administrator set minimum financial requirements, such as net worth or a surety bond, for advisers, often higher for those with custody or discretion.'),
("Before entering a final order suspending an adviser's registration, the Administrator must give:", 'Notice, a hearing chance, findings', ['Only an SEC referral', 'A jury trial', 'Nothing, if the facts are clear'], 'A final order to deny, suspend or revoke a registration requires appropriate notice, an opportunity for a hearing, and written findings of fact and conclusions of law.'),
('A client paid an unregistered adviser for advice and lost money following it. Under the Uniform Securities Act of 2002, she may recover:', 'Fees and losses, less income', ['Only half the fees she paid', 'Triple the advisory fee', 'Nothing, as no sale occurred'], "The 2002 Act lets a person who received advice in violation sue the adviser for the fees paid and losses from the advice, plus interest, costs and attorney's fees, less income received."),
('An investor who has a civil claim under the Act dies before suing. The claim:', 'Survives the investor', ['Ends with her death', 'Passes to the state', 'Becomes a criminal case'], 'Under the Uniform Securities Act, every cause of action survives the death of any person who might have been a plaintiff or defendant.'),
('In a registration by qualification, the Administrator may require that:', 'A prospectus go to each buyer', ['The SEC first approve it', 'The issuer list on an exchange', 'Only accredited investors buy'], 'Under qualification the Administrator controls the filing and may require a prospectus to be sent to each purchaser before or with the sale.'),
('For a speculative new offering registered by qualification, the Administrator may protect buyers by requiring:', "Promoters' shares held in escrow", ['A guaranteed return for buyers', 'A listing on a national exchange', 'Insurance from the FDIC'], 'The Act lets the Administrator require that securities issued to promoters for little cash be put in escrow, and that proceeds be impounded until a minimum is raised.'),
("An agent sells a stock without mentioning a pending lawsuit that threatens the issuer's main product. This is:", 'Omitting a material fact', ['Fine if no question was asked', 'A minor sales tactic', 'Allowed for listed stocks'], 'The antifraud provisions bar untrue statements and omissions of material facts that a reasonable investor would want to know, whether or not the security is registered.'),
('A newspaper published in State A has had more than two-thirds of its circulation outside State A over the past year. An offer printed in it is:', 'Not an offer made in State A', ['An offer made only in State A', 'An offer made in every state', 'An exempt offer everywhere'], 'Under the Uniform Securities Act, an offer in a newspaper published outside the state, or published in the state with more than two-thirds of its circulation outside it over the past 12 months, is not an offer made in that state.'),
]
